+-      Image Sensor: OmniVision ov16a10(16M)(FF)(RAW)(MIPI)
+-
+-so the data this tree publishes in /proc/driver/FrontCamera_info is fine and
+-the failure is inside the kernel.  The kernel log says which part:
+-
+-  CAM_ERR: CAM-EEPROM: cam_eeprom_parse_dt_memory_map: 134 failed: num-blocks
+-    not available rc -22
+-
+-No eeprom subdevice is created for either camera, and the front sensor, which
+-the Hal probes together with its eeprom (cam-sensor@1 has eeprom-src), is then
+-dropped.
+-
+-The reason is a property name mismatch.  The factory device tree names every
+-memory map property with a qcom, prefix:
+-
+-    qcom,num-blocks = <0x02>;
+-    qcom,page0 = ...; qcom,poll0 = ...; qcom,mem0 = ...;
+-    qcom,page1 = ...; qcom,poll1 = ...; qcom,mem1 = ...;
+-
+-and the rest of this camera stack reads its device tree the same way
+-(qcom,cam-power-seq-type, qcom,cam-power-seq-cfg-val, qcom,slave-addr,
+-qcom,i2c-freq-mode).  cam_eeprom_parse_dt_memory_map, however, looked the six
+-names up unprefixed - num-blocks, page%d, pageen%d, saddr%d, poll%d, mem%d - so
+-it failed on the first one and tore the whole map down.
+-
+-Try the prefixed name first and fall back to the bare one, which keeps both this
+-device tree and an upstream one working.
+-
